# The capital's peers at home (new maps round 2, 2026-11-11; docs/maps-plan.md, batch P1). On a capital break the
# peers who live in the city are at home (their break schedules, content/world/cast.grim), and a visit to their
# house shows the private side of them: Lyra at Vaneshade House, Mir at the Ten Clockworks, Corvin on Ashby Lane,
# Seraphine at her mother's rooms, Bram at Hollis House. Each home has one recurring card (`repeat:`, its showings
# keyed in sequence) and a few one-time scenes; each unlocks a fact (content/world/facts/). The pool
# `capital_peers_out` is meeting them out in the city, once each. Families: lore/soft/people.grim.

# ---------------- Lyra: Vaneshade House ----------------

@pool vaneshade_house_lyra
place: vaneshade_house
actor: lyra
class: routine
weight: 12
tags: home social

@event vaneshade_practice_room
title: The Room Upstairs
pool: vaneshade_house_lyra
when: rel(lyra) > -10
repeat: 3
tone: warm
art: flame
scene: A tall bare practice room in a grand house, the floor scorched in rings, a narrow camp bed and a stack of books in one corner, a young woman in duelling clothes.
text: {The footman takes you not to a drawing room but up two flights, to a long bare room with a scorched floor. Lyra is practising. In the corner are a camp bed, a stack of books and one candle. "My bedroom," she says, following your eyes, "is downstairs. It's very nice. Visitors like it."|Up the two flights again to the scorched room. Lyra is already in her duelling clothes{rel(lyra) < 10: , and looks at you as if you were a new kind of target|}. "You came back," she says. "Nobody comes back up here."|The practice room again. Lyra has pushed the camp bed against the wall to make room for two duellists, which she says is for the footwork. "Well?" she says, rolling up her sleeves.}
> left: Spar with her
  vigor +small
  health -small
  rel lyra +tiny
  say: {She doesn't hold back, so neither do you. An hour later you're both sitting on the scorched floor, singed at the cuffs and aching. "Nobody comes up here," she says. "Mother says the smell gets into the curtains."|She opens with the family flourish and then, halfway, drops it for something of her own. You both end up bruised and breathless on the floor. "Don't tell anyone I improvise," she says.|You spar until the candle's out and your arms shake, across the whole cleared floor. At the door she says, "Same time tomorrow," and it isn't a question.}
> right: Ask about the house
  rel lyra +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {"Because downstairs I can hear the house," she says at last, when you ask why she sleeps up here. "Everyone asleep at exactly the right time." She straightens the blanket on the camp bed, which is already straight. You go down feeling the house's quiet too.|She tells you which floorboards her mother uses and which doors are always shut. It's a map of a house, and also of a person. You go down the stairs feeling the weight of all those shut doors.|She asks about your home instead: what you eat, who talks at supper, whether anyone ever shouts. She listens as if it were a lecture. You go down the stairs a little sad for her, and fond.}

@event vaneshade_kitchen_toast
title: Nineteen Years of Toast
pool: vaneshade_house_lyra
when: rel(lyra) >= 12
tone: warm
art: meal
scene: A warm basement kitchen in a great house, copper pans on the walls, a stout grey-haired cook at the range holding a toasting fork, a young noblewoman perched on a kitchen stool.
text: Lyra takes you down the servants' stair "as a shortcut", which it isn't. In the kitchen a stout grey-haired woman is already holding out a plate of toast. "Mrs Callow," Lyra says, sitting on a stool as if she's done it all her life. Mrs Callow looks you up and down. "So you're the one in the letters."
> left: Ask Mrs Callow for stories
  mood +small
  rel lyra -tiny
  say: Mrs Callow tells you about the seven-year-old who set fire to the pudding to prove she could, and the night before her first duel. Lyra says "Mrs Callow" in a warning voice four times. The fourth time she's almost laughing, and still mortified.
> right: Ask about the letters
  rel lyra +small
  say: Mrs Callow fetches a bundle from the dresser drawer: Lyra's letters, one a week since she went away to school at eleven, in a very tidy hand. "About the food, mostly," she says. "And lately, you." Lyra takes the bundle off her, scarlet, puts it back in the drawer, and doesn't deny a word.

@event vaneshade_supper_alone
title: A Table for Twenty
pool: vaneshade_house_lyra
when: rel(lyra) >= 15
slot: evening
tone: warm
art: candle
scene: A long candlelit dining table set for twenty in a dark formal room, a single young woman in black sitting at the far end before one plate.
text: Lady Vaneshade is dining out, as she does every night of the season. Supper at Vaneshade House is served anyway: twenty chairs, eleven courses, one plate. Lyra sits at the end of it like a sentry. "You can stay," she says, "if you like. It's very good. It's always very good."
> left: Stay for all eleven courses
  rel lyra +small
  health +tiny
  mood -small
  say: Two footmen bring a chair to set beside hers, which has plainly never been done. Eleven courses, mostly in silence, and a long, stiff evening. By the pudding she's explaining the sauce, and her shoulders are down. At the door she says it was adequate, and then winces.
> right: Take her out for a pie
  gold -small
  mood +small
  rel lyra +small
  say: You buy two hot pies from a stall on the bridges and eat them leaning on the parapet. Lyra has never eaten anything standing in the street. She gets gravy on her glove and stares at it like a new spell. "Don't tell my mother," she says.

@event vaneshade_portrait_gallery
title: The One Who Laughed
pool: vaneshade_house_lyra
when: rel(lyra) >= 18
tone: warm
art: crown
scene: A long gallery of stern dark portraits of nobles in black, and one painting of a laughing old woman with a small flame dancing in her palm, a young woman standing before it.
text: The long gallery at Vaneshade House is two hundred years of black coats and unsmiling mouths. Lyra walks it fast, until the last portrait: an old woman in black, laughing, a little flame curled in her palm like a cat. Lyra stops. "My grandmother," she says. "She was never adequate at anything. She was wonderful."
> left: Ask what she was like
  rel lyra +small
  mood +tiny
  say: "She lit candles by telling them jokes," Lyra says. "Bad ones. Fire likes to be surprised, she said." She tells you three. They're terrible. {knows(lyra.wildfire): "Wildfire was hers," she adds. "You know that. You're the only one who does."|She doesn't say what else her grandmother left her, but you can tell there's something.}
> right: Light a candle her way
  arcana +small
  mana -tiny
  rel lyra +tiny
  say: You tell the candle on the side table the worst joke you know and push a little magic after it. Nothing. Lyra snorts, which she never does, and the wick catches. "It was laughing at you," she says. "That counts. She always said that counts."

# ---------------- Mir: the Ten Clockworks ----------------

@pool ten_clockworks_mir
place: ten_clockworks
actor: mir
class: routine
weight: 12
tags: home social

@event clockworks_long_bench
title: Properly
pool: ten_clockworks_mir
repeat: 3
tone: light
art: gear
scene: A long workbench in a clock workshop, a family of artificers in aprons working in silence, a young person with soot-smudged goggles bent very still over a tiny gear.
text: {At the Ten Clockworks the whole family works at one long bench, in silence, and nobody has set anything on fire for years. Mir is at the end of it, mending a duchess's carriage clock. Properly. Slowly. Without a single exclamation mark. They look up, see you, and their face lights like a fuse.|Back at the long bench. Mir has moved their stool an inch further from the rest, which in this house is a rebellion. "Pass the three-tooth," they whisper: the first word anyone has spoken in an hour.|The bench again, but today Mir's father has set a second stool beside his own, and Mir is on it, and he is watching their hands. Mir has never looked so frightened, or so pleased.}
> left: Work beside them, properly
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  say: {Three hours, one gear, no talking. Mir's father nods at your work once, which Mir says later is "basically a parade". Your back aches and your eyes swim, and you understand Mir a little better. They can work this way. They just can't breathe in it.|Two hours on a mainspring that won't sit, until your neck locks. Mir finishes theirs, then yours, under the bench, so their father won't see. "Properly," they mouth at you, and grin.|You sit very still while Mir's father talks them through an escapement, slowly, the way he talks to clocks. Mir says after that it's the most he has said to them all year. Your back aches from not moving. It's worth every minute.}
> right: Ask to see their real workshop
  rel mir +small
  health -tiny
  say: {The real workshop is a cupboard under the attic stairs, with a stool, a lamp, a drawer of borrowed springs and a scorch mark on the ceiling shaped like a bird. "First fire," Mir says proudly. You hit your head twice on the way out. "Everyone does," they say. "It's character-building."|In the cupboard under the attic stairs, Mir shows you this break's project: a clock that runs backwards when it's sad. You crack your head on the lintel. "Character," Mir says sympathetically. "Everyone does it."|Mir has pinned a drawing inside the cupboard door: the long bench, with a small fire at one end of it. "Someday," they say. You bang your elbow getting out, and they laugh, and it's a good laugh.}

@event clockworks_drawer
title: The Drawer Marked M.
pool: ten_clockworks_mir
when: rel(mir) >= 15
tone: warm
art: gear
scene: A clockmaker's study, a tall cabinet of narrow drawers, one drawer pulled out to show small, carefully oiled childish contraptions with neat paper labels.
text: Mir's father sends you up to his study for a jar of oil. There's a cabinet of narrow drawers, and one, marked only "M.", is open a crack. Inside, oiled and tagged in his neat hand, are a tin bird, a wind-up beetle, a spoon that stirs by itself and dozens more: everything Mir ever made as a child.
> left: Tell Mir what you found
  rel mir +small
  mood -tiny
  say: Mir goes up without a word and comes back an hour later with red eyes and the tin bird, wound up and singing. "He labelled the beetle 'first walking mechanism, age six'," they say. "He wrote it down." They're quiet all evening, and so are you, and it's heavy and good.
> right: Shut it and say nothing
  guile +small
  mood +tiny
  say: You ease the drawer shut and take the oil down. At supper Mir's father asks them, gruffly, what they're working on at the academy, and listens to the whole answer, every tangent. You catch his eye once. He looks away first.

@event clockworks_greta
title: The Proper One
pool: ten_clockworks_mir
tone: light
art: gear
scene: Two siblings at a clockmaker's bench, an older woman in a spotless apron with tweezers and a younger artificer with singed sleeves, both looking up at a guest.
text: Greta Ten is Mir's older sister, the one who'll have the clockworks, and she's very good at it: spotless apron, steady hands, a clock in a hundred pieces that she'll have together by supper. "So you're the friend," she says. "Tell me honestly. Do they really set things on fire up there? On purpose?"
> left: Tell her about the fires
  charm +small
  rel mir -tiny
  say: You tell her about the owl, the ornithopter and the cauldron that stirs itself. Greta laughs until she has to put down her tweezers, while Mir goes mortified, proud, mortified. On the stairs later she says quietly, "I'd trade the lot for one small fire. Don't tell them."
> right: Say Mir's the best you know
  rel mir +small
  mood -tiny
  say: "I know," Greta says, a little stiffly, and sets a gear without looking up, and the room goes awkward. Mir is pink to the ears. That evening Greta asks Mir to look at a stuck escapement, and Mir fixes it in a way that makes her say "Oh," and then, "Show me again."

# ---------------- Corvin: Ashby Lane ----------------

@pool ashby_lane_corvin
place: ashby_lane
actor: corvin
class: routine
weight: 12
tags: home social

@event ashby_the_chair
title: The Chair on the Step
pool: ashby_lane_corvin
repeat: 3
slot: evening
tone: warm
art: cards
scene: A crooked harbour-town lane at dusk, washing strung between leaning houses, an old wooden chair on a worn doorstep and a young man in it shuffling cards.
text: {Halfway down Ashby Lane there's an old wooden chair on a doorstep, and Corvin is in it, shuffling. "My grandad's," he says. "Nobody's sat in it since. Nobody but me." Three neighbours call his name from three windows. None of them sounds pleased. All of them sound fond.|Corvin's in the chair again, dealing patience to nobody. "You found the lane twice," he says. "That's twice more than most." A basket comes down from a window on a string; he drops a coin in it without looking up.|The chair, the step, the cards. Tonight there's a stool beside the chair, hauled out from somewhere, and Corvin pats it. "Lane says you can sit," he says. "Don't let it go to your head."}
> left: Ask about the old man
  rel corvin +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {"He could find a lost thing in any house," Corvin says. "People still knock and ask me where they've put things. Me. As if it ran in the blood." "Does it?" you ask. "Sometimes," he says, and doesn't joke, and the quiet that follows is a sad one, until the lamps come on.|"He said a lost thing wants to be found," Corvin says. "You just stand where it'd look for you." He's quiet a while. You don't push, and the quiet goes sad and comfortable at once.|He tells you the old man's last find: Corvin, aged ten, on a roof, in the rain, though nobody had told him where to look. "Never said how," Corvin says. "Took it with him." You sit with that, sad and close, until the lamps.}
> right: Meet the neighbours
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {The neighbours come down to inspect you, and they're thorough about it. Corvin owes one of them a crown and another a cat; it's complicated. They tell you he was a terrible child. When the watch walks past, all three step in front of him without a word, and the watch walks on.|The neighbours have opinions about you now. One wants to know your prospects; one wants to sell you a hen. It's exhausting. As you go, the hen woman tells Corvin you'll do.|A neighbour brings out a plate of something fried and very hard to chew, and asks after your whole family while you chew it. You pass, wearily. "That's you on the lane, then," Corvin says, as if you'd been sworn in.}

@event ashby_coins_on_the_sill
title: Coins on the Sill
pool: ashby_lane_corvin
when: rel(corvin) >= 12
slot: night
tone: warm
art: coin
scene: A dark harbour lane at night, a young man reaching up to leave a small stack of coins on the sill of a lit window, a student watching from the shadows.
text: Late, Corvin walks you to the end of the lane and stops under a lit window. Inside, a woman is folding someone else's washing. He reaches up, sets a little stack of coins on the sill, quiet as a cat, and steps back into the dark. "My mother," he says. "She doesn't ask where it comes from. I don't go in."
> left: Ask why he doesn't go in
  rel corvin +small
  mood -small
  say: For a long while he says nothing. "Because then she'd ask how I am," he says, "and I'd tell her, and she'd cry, and then I'd make a joke." He makes one now, about windowsills. It isn't funny. You walk back in a heavy silence, the best you've had with him.
> right: Add a coin of your own
  gold -small
  alignment +tiny
  rel corvin +tiny
  say: You set a coin of your own beside his. He looks at it, and at you, and for once has nothing at all to say. Upstairs the woman comes to the window, sees the sill, and stands very still. Corvin pulls you round the corner before she can look down.

@event ashby_lost_ring
title: Like His Grandad
pool: ashby_lane_corvin
when: seen(ashby_the_chair) > 0
slot: evening
tone: light
art: ring
scene: A cramped harbour-town kitchen, an anxious older woman wringing her hands, a lean young man looking slowly round the room, a student lifting the lid of a flour jar.
text: A neighbour comes down the lane at a half-run: her wedding ring is gone, forty years on her finger and gone. "Find it, Corvin," she says. "Like your grandad would." Corvin goes still, then follows her in, and stands in her kitchen without touching anything, just looking. "Help me look," he says to you. "Or don't. Watch."
> left: Search the kitchen yourself
  challenge research using guile apprentice:
    win:
      guile +small
      gold +tiny
      say: You think about where hands go when they're busy, and lift the lid of the flour jar. The ring is in the flour. The neighbour cries, kisses you on both cheeks and presses a coin on you. Corvin looks at you a long moment. "Huh," he says. "Maybe it's catching."
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      say: You turn out every drawer and find string, spoons and a mouse, and feel foolish. Corvin, who hasn't moved, says "Flour jar," and it is. The neighbour kisses him. You get a smaller kiss, for trying, and a broom.
> right: Watch him work
  rel corvin +small
  say: He looks a long time, then says, "You were baking yesterday," and goes to the flour jar, and there it is. Outside, he breathes out. "So that's how he did it," he says. "Watching. All those years I thought it was magic." He grins, shaky. "Don't tell. They like it better as magic."

# ---------------- Seraphine: the Oracle's Rooms ----------------

@pool oracle_rooms_seraphine
place: oracle_rooms
actor: seraphine
class: routine
weight: 12
tags: home magic

@event oracle_window_seat
title: A Year From Now
pool: oracle_rooms_seraphine
repeat: 3
tone: eerie
art: eye
scene: A tall window seat at the top of a hushed staircase, a dreamy young woman with a sketchbook painting the anxious people queuing on the stairs below.
text: {Seraphine is in the window seat at the top of her mother's stairs, painting the queue. Not as they are, you see over her shoulder; the man in the green coat is greyer, the girl with the letter is laughing. "A year from now," she says, "more or less. They come to hear what they want. I paint what they'll get."|Seraphine's in the window seat again, the sketchbook open at a new face: a woman near the top of the queue clutching a ring box. "She'll get the answer she wants," Seraphine says, "and the one she needs a little after. Same man." She sounds pleased.|The queue is longer this week; there's a duke in it. Seraphine paints him from the back, which is all of him she'll look at. "His future's very loud," she says. "Lots of horses. Sit there, would you? You're quiet."}
> left: Ask about one of them
  rel seraphine +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {"The man in green has come to ask if his ship comes in," she says. "It doesn't. Mother will tell him it does." She paints a little boat in the corner of his page, sailing off. "He'll be all right. Greyer. He'll open a shop." You watch him, so hopeful, and feel the year coming for him.|"She'll say no to him," Seraphine says of the ring-box woman, "and then, later, yes, and they'll both be glad she waited." She paints a small door in the corner. You watch the woman, so sure of herself, and feel uneasy for her.|"He'll lose a horse race and win a wife," Seraphine says of the duke, "and be cross about the horse." She paints a hoofprint. You laugh, and then don't, because he has turned and is looking straight up at you, and you feel caught out.}
> right: Help her paint the stairs
  mood +small
  rel seraphine +tiny
  say: {She hands you a brush and lets you do the stairs, which will be the same next year. You do them badly. She says they're the best stairs anyone's painted for her, and adds a small figure at the top of them, which is you, a year older, holding a door open.|You do the banisters, carefully, and not too badly. She adds a cat on the landing that isn't there. "There will be," she says.|You paint the window you're both sitting in, and her in it. She looks at your picture a long time. "So that's what I look like from outside," she says, and keeps it.}

@event oracle_audience
title: An Audience
pool: oracle_rooms_seraphine
when: rel(seraphine) >= 12
tone: tense
art: eye
scene: A dim, rich audience room with heavy curtains, a regal woman in silver seated at a table with a single candle, eyes half-closed, a nervous student opposite her.
text: The Oracle of Vell sees you because her daughter asked, which, the clerk murmurs, has never happened. She is all silver and stillness, and doesn't look at you but a little to your left, where you'll stand next week. "She listens to you," she says. "Tell her to come home and take her seat. Kings will be grateful. So will I."
> left: Promise to persuade her
  renown +small
  rel seraphine -small
  alignment -tiny
  say: The Oracle smiles exactly as much as she means to, and by evening two uptown houses have heard you're a friend of the family. Seraphine, on the stairs, looks at you once and knows; she always knows. "She asked you too," she says. "Everyone says yes to her. It's all right." It isn't, quite.
> right: Refuse, to her face
  rel seraphine +small
  mood -small
  say: "No," you say. The Oracle turns her eyes on you for one cold moment, and you feel weighed, and found wanting. "I saw you say that," she says. "I still had to ask." The curtains close. Seraphine is waiting on the stairs, delighted. "Nobody says no to her," she says. "She'll be cross for a week."

@event oracle_other_painting
title: The Other Ending
pool: oracle_rooms_seraphine
when: rel(seraphine) >= 18
tone: warm
art: mirror
scene: A young woman kneeling to lift the cushion of a window seat, a hidden canvas beneath it showing herself turning away from a tall silver chair.
text: When the last petitioner has gone, Seraphine lifts the cushion of the window seat. Underneath is a canvas, face down. She turns it over: herself, older, turning away from a tall silver chair. "Mother has seen me say yes," she says. "{chosen(seraphine_mother, left): Even after I wrote no, she still sees yes.|She always sees me say yes.} So I painted the other one. In case it helps."
> left: "It helps."
  rel seraphine +small
  lore +tiny
  say: She looks at the painting as if it were someone she's going to meet. "If I look at it long enough," she says, "it might start to look like a future instead of a wish." She slides it back under the cushion. "Stay a bit longer." You do, until the room goes dark.
> right: "Paint it bigger."
  gold -small
  mood +small
  rel seraphine +small
  say: Next morning you spend more than you should on the biggest canvas in the capital and carry it up the Oracle's stairs past the whole astonished queue. Seraphine laughs, a real laugh, the kind that makes the clerk look up. "It won't fit under the seat," she says. "Good."

# ---------------- Bram: Hollis House ----------------

@pool hollis_house_bram
place: hollis_house
actor: bram
class: routine
weight: 12
tags: home social

@event hollis_inspection
title: Boots
pool: hollis_house_bram
when: rel(bram) > -10
repeat: 3
tone: tense
art: shield
scene: A narrow, immaculate entrance hall, a stern tall man in an old prefect's sash inspecting a burly young man standing at attention, a guest waiting by the door.
text: {In the hall at Hollis House, Bram stands at attention while his father inspects his boots, his collar and his fingernails, exactly as Bram inspects first-years. Bram says "sir" and nothing else; the demerit ledger is in his breast pocket, like a stone. Then Mr Hollis turns to you. "Boots," he says.|Inspection again. Mr Hollis finds a crease in Bram's collar and looks at it for a long, silent moment. Bram stares at the wall. Then the eyes move to you, and to your boots, which you polished, which isn't enough.|The hall, the boots, the silence. This time when his father turns to you, Bram, staring straight ahead, steps half a pace sideways, so that he's standing a little in front of you.}
> left: Hold out your boots
  rel bram +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {Mr Hollis examines your boots as if they had lied to him. "Passable, for a guest," he says, and goes. Bram lets out a breath you hadn't heard him holding. "Every day he's home," he says. "Since I was six." You feel scrubbed and small. So, you realise, does he.|"Scuffed at the heel," Mr Hollis says, and walks away, and your face burns. Bram, very quietly, hands you a polishing cloth from his pocket. He carries one. Of course he carries one.|Mr Hollis looks at the boots, then at his son half in front of you, for a long time. "Hm," he says, and goes. Bram doesn't move until the study door shuts, and your heart is going like a drum. "Don't," he says. "Don't make it weird."}
> right: Ask about his head-boy year
  charm +small
  mood -small
  say: {Mr Hollis's face changes, and he talks about his year for an hour, every prize, while you stand there bored to tears and Bram slips away to the kitchen. Later Bram gives you an apple, which from him is a medal.|He talks for an hour about his rival for head boy, whom he has never forgiven, and your attention dies twice. Bram, freed, has polished both your boots by the time you get away.|Today Mr Hollis talks about his own father, and stops halfway, and goes into the study. Bram stares at the shut door. "He always stops there," he says. The long hour has left you tired and strange.}

@event hollis_noon_piano
title: Scales at Noon
pool: hollis_house_bram
slot: noon
tone: light
art: music
scene: A tidy uptown parlour at midday, a burly young man at an upright piano playing scales with grim precision, a startled guest in the doorway.
text: The maid shows you into the parlour at noon and leaves before you can stop her. Bram is at the piano, playing scales: up, down, perfectly in time, with the face he uses for writing demerits. He sees you and stops dead. "My father played," he says. "I was told to start at six. Nobody said I could stop."
> left: Ask him to play something real
  mood +small
  rel bram +tiny
  say: He glares at you for a whole bar. Then he plays a country dance tune, badly on purpose and then not badly at all. Halfway through he almost smiles. At the end he shuts the lid. "If this is in the Ledger," he says, "I'll know who."
> right: Pretend you heard nothing
  rel bram +small
  mood -tiny
  say: "Heard what?" you say, and talk about the weather, awkwardly and at length, until his ears stop being red. He shows you out himself. On the step he says, gruffly, that you can come at noon again if you like. "For the weather," he says.

@event hollis_hester
title: The Hollis Who Got Away
pool: hollis_house_bram
tone: light
art: house
scene: A narrow hallway in a very correct house, a sharp-faced young woman in a Thornwick scarf eating an apple on the stairs, a burly younger brother looking caught, a guest by the door.
text: A young woman in a Thornwick scarf is sitting on the stairs of Hollis House eating an apple, while upstairs a door stays loudly shut. "Hester," she says, holding out a sticky hand. "The family disgrace. Thornwick, you know. Father's not speaking to me, so Bram's speaking for two." Bram looks as if he'd like the stairs to swallow him.
> left: Sit with Hester
  charm +small
  rel bram -tiny
  say: Hester tells you Bram has written to her every month since she left, and keeps her letters in "that ridiculous ledger". Bram says "Hester" in a voice that could stop a duel, and glares at you for listening. She ruffles his hair. He lets her. Nobody ruffles Bram's hair and lives.
> right: Get Bram out of the house
  rel bram +small
  health -tiny
  say: You invent an errand, and Bram seizes it like a rope. You walk the whole length of the Crescent without a word, until your feet ache and he says, "She's the brave one. Don't tell her I said so." Then: "Don't tell him I write to her." You don't ask who. You don't need to.

# ---------------- Out in the city ----------------

@pool capital_peers_out
region: capital
class: routine
weight: 8
tags: social

@event lyra_capital_promenade
title: Being Seen
pool: capital_peers_out
actor: lyra
when: met(lyra)
tone: light
art: flower
scene: A fashionable city street, well-dressed people strolling slowly, a proud young woman in black walking between two older ladies and glancing at a student.
text: Lyra comes down the street between two of her mother's friends, all three walking slowly to be seen, the uptown way. She looks bored in a very expensive manner. Then she sees you. {rel(lyra) >= 10: For a moment her face does something unguarded.|Her chin goes up a degree.} The ladies would like to know who you are.
> left: Bow and play along
  charm +small
  renown +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: You bow exactly as low as you should, and keep it up, stiffly, for a whole street. The ladies approve of you by a quarter-inch. Lyra introduces you as "a colleague" and gives you a look that promises an explanation later. A note comes instead, which is more.
> right: Ask her to walk with you
  rel lyra +small
  renown -tiny
  say: {rel(lyra) >= 0: The ladies stare. Lyra hesitates exactly one breath, then excuses herself "to discuss duelling". You walk three streets before she speaks. "They'll tell my mother," she says, and sounds, for once, as if she doesn't care.|"Certainly not," she says, and then does, to annoy them. The ladies talk about you all the way home. You argue about duelling for three streets, and it's the best she's looked all week.}

@event mir_capital_brass
title: Brass by the Pound
pool: capital_peers_out
actor: mir
when: met(mir)
tone: light
art: gear
scene: A busy city street, a beaming young artificer with soot-smudged goggles carrying an overflowing sack of scrap brass on one shoulder.
text: Mir comes at you down the street with a sack of brass on one shoulder and a look of pure joy. "The bazaar sells scrap by the pound," they say. "By the pound! I've spent my coach fare. Probably I've spent next month. Look at this escapement, though. Look at it."
> left: Pay their fare home
  gold -small
  rel mir +small
  say: You pay their coach fare home, and see the coins go with a small pang. A week later they give you a little brass beetle "for the fare", which walks in circles and, if you hum, in squares. They never explain the squares.
> right: Help carry it home
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: It weighs more than Mir does. You carry it across half the city to the works quarter, swapping shoulders every street, while Mir explains what each piece will become. By the Ten Clockworks you know a great deal about escapements and nothing about your left arm.

@event bram_capital_no_sash
title: Off Duty
pool: capital_peers_out
actor: bram
when: met(bram)
tone: light
art: scroll
scene: A busy city street, a burly young man without his prefect's sash looking lost, a fat pigeon eating a dropped pie at his feet, a notebook in his hand.
text: Bram Hollis on holiday looks like a door without a frame. You find him in the street, staring at a pigeon that is eating somebody's dropped pie in a plainly unauthorised manner. His hand keeps going to the ledger in his pocket. "There are no rules here," he says, appalled. "Nobody's in charge of any of it."
> left: Help him write up the pigeon
  mood +small
  renown -tiny
  say: You stand guard while Bram writes the pigeon a demerit "for loitering and theft of pastry" and tucks it under a stone. A small crowd gathers and stares at you both. Someone applauds. Bram goes red and then, to his own visible surprise, laughs.
> right: Show him the city's rules
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  say: You walk him through the lamplighters' rounds, the cab ranks, the order of the fish-market stalls: every street has rules nobody wrote down. By the bridges your feet ache, and Bram is calmer and taking notes. "Unofficial," he says. "But consistent. I can respect that."

# Two of them have a place they're found, at the hour their schedules put them there.

@event corvin_capital_quay
title: Good Hands
place: capital_fish_market
actor: corvin
class: routine
weight: 12
slot: noon
when: met(corvin)
tone: warm
art: hand
tags: social
scene: A busy fish market on a stone quay at midday, a young dockhand slipping a purse from a stranger's coat, a lean young man watching her with an odd expression, a student beside him.
text: In the crowd at the fish market Corvin goes suddenly still. A young dockhand is drifting along the stalls, light-fingered: a purse here, a handkerchief there. "Good hands," Corvin murmurs. "Bad habits. She'll be caught before the year's out." He doesn't take his eyes off her. "I'm going to have a word. Coming?"
> left: Go with him
  rel corvin +small
  health +tiny
  say: Corvin falls into step beside her and talks low and fast, as you've never heard him talk: no jokes, a lot of street names. She looks at him as if he were a ghost, and goes. "Somebody did that for me once," he says. "I didn't listen either." Then he buys you both fried fish.
> right: Watch how she works
  guile +small
  alignment -tiny
  rel corvin -tiny
  say: You stay by the stalls and study her, a thief's lesson taken for free: the bump, the apology, the hand already gone. You learn more in ten minutes than in a term. Corvin comes back alone, quiet, and a little cool with you for staying behind. He won't say more.

@event seraphine_capital_bells
title: Before the Bells
place: capital_cathedral
actor: seraphine
class: routine
weight: 12
slot: evening
when: met(seraphine)
tone: light
art: bell
tags: social magic
scene: The wide steps of a cathedral at dusk, a dreamy young woman sitting among a ring of cab drivers holding out coins, the great spire above.
text: On the steps of the Cathedral of the Lamp, Seraphine is taking bets from a ring of cab drivers on exactly when the next bell will ring. She's winning. She's been winning for an hour. "I hear them early," she tells you, quite untroubled. "All the bells, all day. It seemed a waste." The drivers are starting to look at each other.
> left: Get her away before they twig
  rel seraphine +small
  alignment +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: You make her give back half and hurry her off, red-faced, while the drivers argue over the rest. "Was that wrong?" she asks three streets later, genuinely curious. "I did tell them I'd win. They didn't believe me. Nobody ever believes the true one."
> right: Bet on her yourself
  gold +small
  alignment -tiny
  say: You put a crown on the quarter-hour and win four. Seraphine squeezes your arm at every bell, a breath early. Then a driver works it out, and you both run down the cathedral steps laughing, pockets jingling, his shouting fading behind you.
